{"title":"2019 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(easier_answer_choice_elimination)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (easier answer choice elimination)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Elimination)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Elimination)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Trying_the_answer_choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Trying the answer choices)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the greatest three-digit positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> for which the sum of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/4/38456c167ef3c34be33530bc07a572b388ae3cbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\underline{\\text{not}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"16\" /> a divisor of the product of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/5/f05adb06f1da4084c8deab20fba721a222658454.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 995 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 996 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 997 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 998 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"443\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/9/1c94c91c9b0a00273b5a22184f70746cdc42ab64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n)(n+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" />, and we want this to not be a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> (the product of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers). Notice that if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> were composite, all of its factors would be less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, which means they would be able to cancel with the factors in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers would be a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> is composite. (Note: This is true for all positive integers except for 1 because 2 is not a divisor/factor of 1.) Hence in this case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> must instead be prime. The greatest three-digit integer that is prime is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/7/90782e3f18f6a430876413c43e3d163e0e8ae3f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$997$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />, so we subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb988d8eb6468bf9f04a1c23d9e8393edff29f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"109\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.1\">Solution 1.1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the first \\( n \\) positive integers starting from 1 is \\( \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\). The product of the first \\( n \\) positive integers starting from \\( n \\), then going to \\( (n-1) \\), all the way down to 1, is \\( n! \\). If there exists a number such that \\( n! / \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = +\\mathbb{Z} \\), then we call that solution invalid. \\( n! / \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\) is just \\( \\frac{2n!}{n(n+1)} \\). We see that the first term of \\( n! \\), which is \\( n \\), will cancel out with the \\( n \\) term on the denominator. In order for \\( n-1 \\) to not cancel out, it needs to be a prime number. The only answer choice that satisfies this <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb988d8eb6468bf9f04a1c23d9e8393edff29f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"109\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation (not the creator of Solution 1)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28easier_answer_choice_elimination.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(easier_answer_choice_elimination)\">Solution 2 (easier answer choice elimination)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (easier answer choice elimination)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in Solution 1, we deduce that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> must be prime. \n</p><p>It's hard to remember all of your primes up to a thousand, so we eliminate answer choices\nchoices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> don't work because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> is even, and all even numbers are divisible by two, which makes choices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> composite and not prime. \n</p><p>Choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> also does not work since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which means it's a composite number and not prime. Thus, the correct answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f849e40466e214dd88b78478ada483cd90ab5681.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"73\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Elimination.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Elimination)\">Solution 3 (Elimination)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Elimination)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive integers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/9/1c94c91c9b0a00273b5a22184f70746cdc42ab64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n)(n+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" /> and the product of the positive integers upto <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. The quotient of the two is -\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/f/4ef6b9eefa24dab8954a26ab3659f6cca6ec8e1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(2)(n!)}{(n)(n+1)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"87\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>which simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/4/36457947afa1b10cb7fd7acb3133a2ec39bf7b50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(2)((n-1)!)}{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"105\" height=\"42\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> must be odd for the remainder to not be 0 (as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will multiply with some number in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, cancelling out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> if it is even, which leaves us with the answer choices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/6/d26afe0faeeb76f9795d6b39d51a43ed9ec8f66c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 996$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/8/4e88e88ead9f55f1646329a34e0b74f732bc7c5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 998$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5429dc4aa7ba1f3b20be4599a34c4220cc79db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> must also be prime as otherwise there will be a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5429dc4aa7ba1f3b20be4599a34c4220cc79db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> x <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> somewhere. So either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/7/90782e3f18f6a430876413c43e3d163e0e8ae3f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$997$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> must be prime - <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is obviously not prime as it is divisible by 9, so our answer should be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d4f8052bdca3e9e72ba64ea9333a9883de7633.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1 = 997$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"96\" height=\"15\" />, and so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/6/d26afe0faeeb76f9795d6b39d51a43ed9ec8f66c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 996$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f849e40466e214dd88b78478ada483cd90ab5681.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"73\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>- youtube.com/indianmathguy\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Trying_the_answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Trying_the_answer_choices)\">Solution 4 (Trying the answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Trying the answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the numbers from 1 through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/9/1c94c91c9b0a00273b5a22184f70746cdc42ab64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n)(n+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" />, and obviously, the products are the factorial; <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. We can try 995 first, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/4/904fbe21de6e3e87e6321048e6f06ce7f117e716.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{995(995+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"40\" /> is the sum of the integers up to 995. This can be simplified to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/5/ea58841f68c519dd00f7b693c188beb769ba836c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$995 \\cdot 498$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"14\" />. Then we can set up an equation stating that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/a/aeabcef00382c3e05d9d76b53263b1dde42c76f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$995! = 995 \\cdot 498 \\cdot \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"152\" height=\"14\" />. (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e6cd0e4103015efba12e0fc4291f012b467eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is a number that would satisfy the equation as a natural number) Then we can cancel out 995 on each side, making it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/2/8a2c175f6e1e950fe7cc49b82d06ec9ab10fa400.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$994! = 498 \\cdot \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"112\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/b/f6bcd6dbce5032a650b1be5b0c5d8f64e157ba0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$498 \\mid 994!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"20\" />. Since 498 is a factor of 994, answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/6/5c627f0d81f50b0bae8e69e10f14705b86ef030f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 995}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"76\" height=\"30\" /> is correct. \n</p><p>So we move on to the next one, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f849e40466e214dd88b78478ada483cd90ab5681.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"73\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/d/3cd7fdae57b99e7ca68040894f7d0b343b3a05a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{996(996+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"40\" /> is the sum, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94cfc7b61fe24ca905b4a8f4d5718a52067cb646.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$996!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" /> is the products. This makes it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/2/8c2a4427c2daf54d3d9295087a2c4de01eda40ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$996! = 498 \\cdot 997 \\cdot \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"152\" height=\"14\" />. But then we can see that 997 is a prime number. In the expression <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94cfc7b61fe24ca905b4a8f4d5718a52067cb646.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$996!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" />, there is no 997 as a factor (since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94cfc7b61fe24ca905b4a8f4d5718a52067cb646.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$996!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/5/1b5d1987ce79c94e7766ea873d906f017fe81ae2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 995 \\cdot 996$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"171\" height=\"14\" />, and there is clearly no 997 as a factor), making it so there is no natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e6cd0e4103015efba12e0fc4291f012b467eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> that satisfies the equation. So, we can conclude the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f849e40466e214dd88b78478ada483cd90ab5681.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 996}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"73\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>-Reddessiner (CCG2)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solutions\">Video Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/2vucE8HTiuU\">https://youtu.be/2vucE8HTiuU</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/FDgcLW4frg8?t=33\">https://youtu.be/FDgcLW4frg8?t=33</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3\">Video Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ikRv_0kNc2w\">https://youtu.be/ikRv_0kNc2w</a>\n</p><p>Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A\">2019 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofprobl